; hark: root-hints = 127.0.10.1
; hark: dnssec-zone = .
; hark: dnssec-zone = example.

SCENARIO_BEGIN answer signed across a proven zone cut must SERVFAIL

; hark has walked root -> example. and PROVEN the cut: it fetched example.'s DS
; from the root and validated example.'s DNSKEY against it. So it holds
; validated evidence that data under example. is example.'s to sign.
;
; The server then answers `www.example. A` carrying the ROOT's signature.
; RFC 4034 §3.1.3 is satisfied (`.` is an ancestor of the owner) and the
; signature verifies against a genuinely trust-anchored key, so hark accepted
; it and set AD=1 — authenticating the parent's word over a child whose
; delegation it had just proven. RFC 4035 §5.3.1 requires the signer to be
; "the name of the zone that contains the RRset", which this is not.
;
; This is BIND's `closer_secure_ds_exists` check (lib/dns/validator.c:255),
; which BIND applies only on the insecurity-proof path.
;
; SERVFAIL, not served-without-AD. The check runs before any DNSKEY fetch, so
; a softer verdict would be a keyless downgrade: the RRSIG would not have to
; verify, only to name an ancestor. See 010 for the control — the same forgery
; signed as the cut zone, which must fail through the crypto path instead.
;
; Topology
;   root     127.0.10.1   delegates example. with a DS
;   example  127.0.10.2   answers for its own zone, but SIGN_AS `.`

RANGE_BEGIN 0 100
  ADDRESS 127.0.10.1

  ENTRY_BEGIN
    MATCH opcode subdomain
    ADJUST copy_id copy_query
    REPLY QR NOERROR
    SECTION QUESTION
      example. IN A
    SECTION AUTHORITY
      example. 3600 IN NS ns.example.
      example. 3600 IN DS PLACEHOLDER
    SECTION ADDITIONAL
      ns.example. 3600 IN A 127.0.10.2
  ENTRY_END
RANGE_END

RANGE_BEGIN 0 100
  ADDRESS 127.0.10.2

  ENTRY_BEGIN
    MATCH opcode qname qtype
    ADJUST copy_id copy_query
    ; The forgery: the child's own data, signed by the parent's key. This
    ; address is deliberately NOT registered as serving `.`, so it could not
    ; answer `. DNSKEY` — hark must never need to ask it, because the root key
    ; it validates against is one it already holds.
    SIGN_AS .
    REPLY QR AA NOERROR
    SECTION QUESTION
      www.example. IN A
    SECTION ANSWER
      www.example. 3600 IN A 192.0.2.66
  ENTRY_END
RANGE_END

STEP 1 QUERY
ENTRY_BEGIN
  REPLY RD DO
  SECTION QUESTION
    www.example. IN A
ENTRY_END

STEP 2 CHECK_ANSWER
ENTRY_BEGIN
  MATCH flags question
  REPLY QR RD RA SERVFAIL
  SECTION QUESTION
    www.example. IN A
ENTRY_END

SCENARIO_END
